    "lawCode": "Add Title 22 Chap 13 §§22-1301 & 22-1302, NYC Ad Cd",
    "lawSection": "New York City Administrative Code",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4257A\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the administrative code of the city of New York, in\nrelation to requiring contracts for a commercial lease\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill ensures that current and future commercial tenants are given a\nlease from a property owner with specific information.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends title 22 of the administrative code of the city of\nNew York by adding a new chapter 13 entitled \"Commercial Lease\nContracts\", which ensures that commercial tenants receive a lease,\ndefines terms within leases, and establishes requirements for commercial\nlease contracts. It also provides that commercial tenants have a right\nto request their start date of tenancy be included or excluded in the\nwritten contract, to request to receive a written contract, including\nprovisions to remain a tenant until they receive a written contract,\nwithhold rent after 30 days without written contract, or to suspend\npayment after 60 days without a written contract.\n\nSection 2 establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn New York City commercial tenants, specifically small businesses, lack\nmeaningful rights and protections as their lease expires. Many small\nbusinesses are currently operating without a written contract on a month\nto month basis. In a city where the majority of tenants are currently\nrent burdened and owning a small business is an additional rent burden,\nwe must ensure that commercial tenants have the most basic of\nprotections such as a right to a lease.\n\nIf a business is in a space and is expected to pay rent to exist in said\nspace, then there should always be a written contract in the business'\nand the property owners' possession that states those terms. Although\nthe New York City Council acknowledges there is a affordability crisis\nfor commercial tenants, they have yet to act on a package of bills that\naddresses protections for commercial tenants.  This bill would take one\nstep forward in addressing one of the many issues that New York City\nsmall businesses are facing.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
